\chapter{The Schema}
\label{ch:schema}

The database is ordinary PostgreSQL~\cite{postgresql}.  Schema changes
live as numbered \texttt{.up.sql} / \texttt{.down.sql} pairs under
\texttt{migrations/} and are applied with
\texttt{cl-migratum}~\cite{cl-migratum}.  The initial migration is
\texttt{20260818120000-create-schema}.
\texttt{20260927120000-candlestick-is-closed} adds
\texttt{is\_closed}.  Statements in those files are separated by
\texttt{--;;} lines, which is how cl-migratum splits a file into steps.

This chapter is the map of that schema.  The Lisp packages in the
chapters that follow are a veneer over these tables.

\section{Conventions}

\begin{itemize}
\item Every table has a version-4 \textsc{Uuid} primary key whose default
  is PostgreSQL's built-in \texttt{gen\_random\_uuid()}.  That function
  lives in \texttt{pg\_catalog}, so it is available from any schema
  without an extension (PostgreSQL~13 or later).
\item Identity of an instrument is that \textsc{Uuid}, not a ticker
  symbol.  Abbreviations go in \texttt{tickers}.
\item Timestamps are \texttt{timestamp with time zone}
  (\texttt{timestamptz} below).  The Lisp side speaks Common Lisp
  universal times.
\item Prices, volume, and adjusted close are \texttt{numeric}.  Postmodern
  hands those back as rationals; the library converts them to floats on
  the way out.
\item A data source is who told us.  An exchange is where it traded.  A
  market in the pair-on-a-venue sense is not a table; it is the bar's
  numerator and denominator plus the exchange.
\item Ticker validity windows are half-open,
  $[\textit{valid from}, \textit{valid to})$.  A trigger forbids two
  overlapping windows for the same source, canonical abbreviation, and
  exchange.  The same abbreviation may be reused across non-overlapping
  eras.
\end{itemize}

\section{How the Tables Fit Together}

There are nine tables.  Four of them are catalogs you seed once:
instrument types, data source kinds, exchanges, and candlestick
durations.  Two of them name the things you care about: instruments
and data sources.  Tickers attach abbreviations.  Retrievals and
candlesticks are the growing facts: one fetch, then the bars that
fetch produced.

\begin{itemize}
\item An instrument may have a type.  A data source may have a kind.
\item A ticker names an instrument at one data source, optionally on one
  exchange, during a validity window.  The same abbreviation at two
  sources, or at two exchanges of one source, can be two instruments.
  The same abbreviation at one source can also mean different
  instruments in non-overlapping eras.
\item A retrieval is one act of fetching from a source, optionally pinned
  to an exchange.
\item A candlestick is the as-traded \textsc{Ohlc} of a numerator
  instrument denominated in a denominator instrument, over one duration,
  at one time, from one retrieval.  The unique key includes the
  retrieval, so two sources --- or two fetches of the same source --- are
  two rows.
\end{itemize}

Figure~\ref{fig:schema-relations} is the same map drawn as foreign keys,
with the candlesticks at the top and the catalogs they rest on cascading
down.  A solid arrow is a required key, from the table that holds the
column to the table it references.  A dashed arrow is nullable: a ticker
or a retrieval may omit an exchange.  A candlestick points at two
instruments, the numerator and the denominator of the price.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\resizebox{\linewidth}{!}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  x=3.7cm,
  y=1.85cm,
  font=\scriptsize\ttfamily,
  >=Stealth,
  tbl/.style={
    draw=black!75,
    rounded corners=1.5pt,
    inner xsep=5pt,
    inner ysep=3.5pt,
    outer sep=1.5pt,
    align=center,
    minimum height=1.65em,
  },
  cat/.style={tbl, fill=black!3},
  ent/.style={tbl, fill=black!8},
  lnk/.style={tbl, fill=black!13},
