Foreign keys:

\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{instruments.instrument\_type\_id} $\rightarrow$
  \texttt{instrument\_types}
\item \texttt{data\_sources.data\_source\_kind\_id} $\rightarrow$
  \texttt{data\_source\_kinds}
\item \texttt{tickers.instrument\_id} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{instruments}
\item \texttt{tickers.data\_source\_id} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{data\_sources}
\item \texttt{tickers.exchange\_id} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{exchanges}
  (nullable)
\item \texttt{data\_retrievals.data\_source\_id} $\rightarrow$
  \texttt{data\_sources}
\item \texttt{data\_retrievals.exchange\_id} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{exchanges}
  (nullable)
\item \texttt{candlesticks.numerator\_id} $\rightarrow$
  \texttt{instruments}
\item \texttt{candlesticks.denominator\_id} $\rightarrow$
  \texttt{instruments}
\item \texttt{candlesticks.duration\_id} $\rightarrow$
  \texttt{candlestick\_durations}
\item \texttt{candlesticks.data\_retrieval\_id} $\rightarrow$
  \texttt{data\_retrievals}
\end{itemize}

\section{Overview}

\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{0.55\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Table & What it holds \\
\midrule
\texttt{instrument\_types} &
  What an instrument is: stock, cryptocurrency, \ldots \\
\texttt{data\_source\_kinds} &
  How we talk to a source: api, website, feed, \ldots \\
\texttt{exchanges} &
  Where it traded: NASDAQ, Binance, Uniswap V3, \ldots \\
\texttt{instruments} &
  One instrument; identity is the \textsc{Uuid}, not a ticker \\
\texttt{data\_sources} &
  Who told us: CoinGecko, Yahoo Finance, Zapper.fi, \ldots \\
\texttt{tickers} &
  Abbreviation a source (and optional exchange) uses, with a validity window \\
\texttt{candlestick\_durations} &
  Short name, long name, and a PostgreSQL interval \\
\texttt{data\_retrievals} &
  One fetch from a source, optionally pinned to an exchange \\
\texttt{candlesticks} &
  One \textsc{Ohlc} bar, linked to a pair, a duration, and a retrieval \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

The Lisp packages that talk to each table:

\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
Table & Package \\
\midrule
\texttt{instrument\_types} & \texttt{candlesticks/instrument-types} \\
\texttt{data\_source\_kinds} & \texttt{candlesticks/data-source-kinds} \\
\texttt{exchanges} & \texttt{candlesticks/exchanges} \\
\texttt{instruments} & \texttt{candlesticks/instruments} \\
\texttt{data\_sources} & \texttt{candlesticks/data-sources} \\
\texttt{tickers} & \texttt{candlesticks/tickers} \\
\texttt{candlestick\_durations} & \texttt{candlesticks/durations} \\
\texttt{data\_retrievals} & \texttt{candlesticks/data-retrievals} \\
\texttt{candlesticks} & \texttt{candlesticks/bars} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

Migrations live in \texttt{candlesticks/migrations}.  Connections live
in \texttt{candlesticks/config}.  The umbrella package
\texttt{candlesticks} re-exports the rest.

\section{The \texttt{instrument\_types} Table}

What an instrument \emph{is}: stock, bond, cryptocurrency, and so on.
This is a different ontology from data source kinds, which classify
\emph{how we talk to a source}.

Names are unique.  The Lisp side trims and down-cases them with
\texttt{canonicalize-slug}, so \texttt{"Cryptocurrency"} and
\texttt{" cryptocurrency "} are the same type.

\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{0.46\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Column & Type & Notes \\
\midrule
\texttt{id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Primary key \\
\texttt{name} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Not null, unique \\
\texttt{description} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Optional \\
\texttt{created\_at} & \texttt{timestamptz} &
  Not null, default \texttt{now()} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

The conventional seed, from
\texttt{ensure-standard-instrument-types}:

\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{0.62\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Name & Description \\
\midrule
\texttt{stock} & Equity listed on an exchange \\
\texttt{bond} & Debt instrument \\
\texttt{commodity} & Fungible physical or futures-traded good \\
\texttt{currency} & Fiat currency \\
\texttt{cryptocurrency} & Native on-chain asset \\
\texttt{index} & Published basket or benchmark, not itself traded \\
\texttt{fund} & Pooled vehicle (mutual fund, ETF, \ldots) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\section{The \texttt{data\_source\_kinds} Table}

How Candlesticks talks to a source: api, website, feed, file, or
manual.  A source row is ``the CoinGecko \textsc{Api} we call'', not a
catalog of everything CoinGecko Inc.\ does, so one kind per source is
enough.

Names are unique and canonicalized the same way as instrument types.

\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{0.46\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Column & Type & Notes \\
\midrule
\texttt{id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Primary key \\
\texttt{name} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Not null, unique \\
\texttt{description} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Optional \\
\texttt{created\_at} & \texttt{timestamptz} &
  Not null, default \texttt{now()} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

The conventional seed, from
\texttt{ensure-standard-data-source-kinds}:

\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{0.62\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Name & Description \\
\midrule
\texttt{api} & Programmatic HTTP/RPC \textsc{Api} \\
\texttt{website} & \textsc{Html} or other page we scrape or parse \\
\texttt{feed} & Push feed (websocket, \textsc{Fix}, \ldots) \\
\texttt{file} & File or dump (\textsc{Csv}, \textsc{Json}, \ldots) \\
\texttt{manual} & Entered or corrected by hand \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\section{The \texttt{exchanges} Table}

Where instruments trade: a stock exchange, a \textsc{Cex}, or a
\textsc{Dex}.  That is not a data source, and not a market in the
pair-on-a-venue sense.

The same protocol on two chains is two exchanges.  Uniswap V3 on
Ethereum and Uniswap V3 on Arbitrum are different rows.  Traditional
venues may carry an \textsc{Iso} 10383 \textsc{Mic}: the four-character
Market Identifier Code from the official registry (\texttt{XNAS} for
\textsc{Nasdaq}, \texttt{XNYS} for \textsc{Nyse}).  The column is
optional and unique when present.  Crypto venues and \textsc{Dex}es
usually leave it null and use \texttt{chain} instead.

\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{0.42\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Column & Type & Notes \\
\midrule
\texttt{id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Primary key \\
\texttt{name} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Canonical slug, not null, unique \\
\texttt{display\_name} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Optional human label \\
\texttt{mic} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Optional \textsc{Iso} 10383 Market Identifier Code;
  unique when present \\
\texttt{chain} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Optional; indexed \\
\texttt{description} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Optional \\
\texttt{created\_at} & \texttt{timestamptz} &
  Not null, default \texttt{now()} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

Index: \texttt{exchanges\_chain\_index} on \texttt{chain}.

The conventional seed, from \texttt{ensure-standard-exchanges}:

{\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
Name & Display name & Venue \\
\midrule
\texttt{nasdaq} & NASDAQ & \textsc{Mic} \texttt{XNAS} \\
\texttt{nyse} & New York Stock Exchange & \textsc{Mic} \texttt{XNYS} \\
\texttt{binance} & Binance & centralized crypto \\
\texttt{coinbase} & Coinbase & centralized crypto \\
\texttt{kraken} & Kraken & centralized crypto \\
\texttt{uniswap-v3-ethereum} & Uniswap V3 (Ethereum) & chain \texttt{ethereum} \\
\texttt{uniswap-v3-arbitrum} & Uniswap V3 (Arbitrum) & chain \texttt{arbitrum} \\
\texttt{uniswap-v2-ethereum} & Uniswap V2 (Ethereum) & chain \texttt{ethereum} \\
\texttt{sushiswap-ethereum} & SushiSwap (Ethereum) & chain \texttt{ethereum} \\
\texttt{curve-ethereum} & Curve (Ethereum) & chain \texttt{ethereum} \\
\texttt{pancakeswap-bsc} & PancakeSwap (\textsc{Bsc}) & chain \texttt{bsc} \\
\texttt{orca-solana} & Orca (Solana) & chain \texttt{solana} \\
\texttt{raydium-solana} & Raydium (Solana) & chain \texttt{solana} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}

Other venues are created on first use.

\section{The \texttt{instruments} Table}

One tradable thing: a stock, a coin, a currency, whatever.  Identity is
the \textsc{Uuid}.  \texttt{name} is a human label such as
\texttt{"Bitcoin"} and is neither unique nor required.  Short market
abbreviations live on \texttt{tickers}, because the same abbreviation
can mean different instruments at different sources.

\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{0.36\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Column & Type & Notes \\
\midrule
\texttt{id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Primary key \\
\texttt{name} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Optional display name, not unique \\
\texttt{instrument\_type\_id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Optional \textsc{Fk} to \texttt{instrument\_types} \\
\texttt{created\_at} & \texttt{timestamptz} &
  Not null, default \texttt{now()} \\
\texttt{updated\_at} & \texttt{timestamptz} &
  Not null, default \texttt{now()} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

Index: \texttt{instruments\_type\_index} on \texttt{instrument\_type\_id}.

\section{The \texttt{data\_sources} Table}

Whoever actually produced the numbers: CoinGecko, Zapper.fi, Yahoo
Finance, a \textsc{Csv} dump, a proprietary feed.  Retrievals point at a
source, and candlesticks point at a retrieval, so every bar can be
traced all the way down.

\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{0.36\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Column & Type & Notes \\
\midrule
\texttt{id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Primary key \\
\texttt{name} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Not null, unique \\
\texttt{data\_source\_kind\_id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Optional \textsc{Fk} to \texttt{data\_source\_kinds} \\
\texttt{created\_at} & \texttt{timestamptz} &
  Not null, default \texttt{now()} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

Index: \texttt{data\_sources\_kind\_index} on
\texttt{data\_source\_kind\_id}.

There is no seeded catalog of sources.  A source row is created the
first time you ingest from it.

\section{The \texttt{tickers} Table}

The abbreviation a data source uses for an instrument, optionally on a
particular exchange.  It is \emph{not} the identity of the instrument.
CoinGecko's aggregated \texttt{BTC} (no exchange) can be Bitcoin while
Yahoo's \texttt{BTC} on \textsc{Nyse} is a different instrument.
Yahoo's \texttt{AAPL} and Bloomberg's \texttt{AAPL} can both point at
the same Apple row.

The same $(\textit{data source}, \textit{canonical symbol},
\textit{exchange})$ may appear more than once, so long as the
validity windows do not overlap.  A missing exchange still counts:
two aggregator tickers for the same symbol at the same source cannot
be valid at the same moment.  The window is half-open,
$[\textit{valid from}, \textit{valid to})$.  An unbounded start is
stored as \texttt{-infinity}; a null \texttt{valid\_to} means the
abbreviation is still in use.

\texttt{symbol} is what the source called it.  \texttt{canonical} is
that abbreviation trimmed and upper-cased, so \texttt{"btc"} and
\texttt{" BTC "} match \emph{within one source}.

\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{0.36\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Column & Type & Notes \\
\midrule
\texttt{id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Primary key \\
\texttt{instrument\_id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Not null, \textsc{Fk} to \texttt{instruments} \\
\texttt{data\_source\_id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Not null, \textsc{Fk} to \texttt{data\_sources} \\
\texttt{exchange\_id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Optional \textsc{Fk} to \texttt{exchanges} \\
\texttt{symbol} & \texttt{varchar} &
  As the source wrote it, not null \\
\texttt{canonical} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Normalized form, not null \\
\texttt{valid\_from} & \texttt{timestamptz} &
  Not null, default \texttt{-infinity} \\
\texttt{valid\_to} & \texttt{timestamptz} &
  Optional; null means still valid \\
\texttt{created\_at} & \texttt{timestamptz} &
  Not null, default \texttt{now()} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

Indexes:
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{tickers\_instrument\_index} on \texttt{instrument\_id}
\item \texttt{tickers\_canonical\_index} on \texttt{canonical}
\item \texttt{tickers\_exchange\_index} on \texttt{exchange\_id}
\item \texttt{tickers\_lookup\_index} on source, canonical, and
  \texttt{valid\_from}
\end{itemize}

A trigger forbids two overlapping windows for the same source,
canonical abbreviation, and exchange.  A check constraint requires
\texttt{valid\_to} to be after \texttt{valid\_from} when both are
present.

\section{The \texttt{candlestick\_durations} Table}

One candlestick time window: a short name (such as \texttt{"w"}), a
long name (such as \texttt{"week"}), and the PostgreSQL interval that
corresponds to it.

Short names are unique on their own, and so are long names --- there is
only one \texttt{"d"} and only one \texttt{"week"}.  That is two unique
constraints, not one unique pair.

\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{0.46\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Column & Type & Notes \\
\midrule
\texttt{id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Primary key \\
\texttt{short\_name} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Not null, unique \\
\texttt{long\_name} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Not null, unique \\
\texttt{duration} & \texttt{interval} &
  Not null \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

The conventional seed, from \texttt{ensure-standard-durations}:

\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
Short & Long & Interval \\
\midrule
\texttt{s} & \texttt{second} & \texttt{1 second} \\
\texttt{m} & \texttt{minute} & \texttt{1 minute} \\
\texttt{h} & \texttt{hour} & \texttt{1 hour} \\
\texttt{d} & \texttt{day} & \texttt{1 day} \\
\texttt{w} & \texttt{week} & \texttt{1 week} \\
\texttt{M} & \texttt{month} & \texttt{1 month} \\
\texttt{y} & \texttt{year} & \texttt{1 year} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

Sources that publish other windows can add their own rows.  Note that
month is \texttt{M} (upper case) so it does not collide with minute.

\section{The \texttt{data\_retrievals} Table}

One act of fetching a set of data from a data source: when it happened,
the endpoint that was queried, and any notes.  Every candlestick points
back to the retrieval that produced it.  An optional
\texttt{exchange\_id} pins the fetch to a venue when the source was
asked about one.

\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{0.36\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Column & Type & Notes \\
\midrule
\texttt{id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Primary key \\
\texttt{data\_source\_id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Not null, \textsc{Fk} to \texttt{data\_sources} \\
\texttt{exchange\_id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Optional \textsc{Fk} to \texttt{exchanges} \\
\texttt{retrieved\_at} & \texttt{timestamptz} &
  Not null, default \texttt{now()} \\
\texttt{endpoint} & \texttt{varchar} &
  Optional \\
\texttt{notes} & \texttt{text} &
  Optional \\
\texttt{created\_at} & \texttt{timestamptz} &
  Not null, default \texttt{now()} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

Indexes:
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{data\_retrievals\_source\_index} on
  \texttt{data\_source\_id}
\item \texttt{data\_retrievals\_exchange\_index} on
  \texttt{exchange\_id}
\item \texttt{data\_retrievals\_retrieved\_index} on
  \texttt{retrieved\_at}
\end{itemize}

\section{The \texttt{candlesticks} Table}

One \textsc{Ohlc} bar.  A price is always a pair of instruments:
\texttt{BTC/USD} is Bitcoin as the numerator and the US Dollar as the
denominator.  Open, high, low, and close are as-traded.
\texttt{adjusted\_close} is the source's split-adjusted close when it
reported one, and may be null.  Volume may be null.
\texttt{is\_closed} is true when the print was taken after the bar's
session ended.  It defaults to true.  The caller decides when the
session ends; a crypto day and a stock session are not the library's
calendar.

The unique key is $(\textit{numerator}$, $\textit{denominator}$,
$\textit{duration}$, $\textit{time}$, $\textit{retrieval})$.  Inserting the
same bar twice against one retrieval is a no-op; ingesting it again
under a new retrieval stores a second instance.

\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{0.38\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Column & Type & Notes \\
\midrule
\texttt{id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Primary key \\
\texttt{numerator\_id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Not null, \textsc{Fk} to \texttt{instruments} \\
\texttt{denominator\_id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Not null, \textsc{Fk} to \texttt{instruments} \\
\texttt{duration\_id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Not null, \textsc{Fk} to \texttt{candlestick\_durations} \\
\texttt{data\_retrieval\_id} & \texttt{uuid} &
  Not null, \textsc{Fk} to \texttt{data\_retrievals} \\
\texttt{time} & \texttt{timestamptz} &
  Not null; the bar's period \\
\texttt{open} & \texttt{numeric} &
  Not null, as-traded \\
\texttt{high} & \texttt{numeric} &
  Not null, as-traded \\
\texttt{low} & \texttt{numeric} &
  Not null, as-traded \\
\texttt{close} & \texttt{numeric} &
  Not null, as-traded \\
\texttt{volume} & \texttt{numeric} &
  Optional \\
\texttt{adjusted\_close} & \texttt{numeric} &
  Optional split-adjusted close \\
\texttt{is\_closed} & \texttt{boolean} &
  Not null, default true.  False while the session is still open \\
\texttt{created\_at} & \texttt{timestamptz} &
  Not null, default \texttt{now()} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

Check constraints:

\begin{itemize}
\item The numerator and the denominator are different instruments.
\item \texttt{high} $\geq$ \texttt{low}.
\item \texttt{high} is at least \texttt{open} and at least \texttt{close}.
\item \texttt{low} is at most \texttt{open} and at most \texttt{close}.
\item \texttt{volume} is null or $\geq 0$.
\item \texttt{adjusted\_close} is null or $\geq 0$.
\end{itemize}

Indexes:
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{candlesticks\_query\_index} on numerator, denominator,
  duration, and time --- the lookup \texttt{get-candlesticks} uses
\item \texttt{candlesticks\_retrieval\_index} on
  \texttt{data\_retrieval\_id}
\end{itemize}

\section{What Is Not a Table}

A market in the pair-on-an-exchange sense is the candlestick's numerator
and denominator plus the exchange.  That can wait; it is not a separate
relation yet.
